1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,813
A script that does the same thing every time is not very useful.

2
00:00:04,813 --> 00:00:07,105
Useful scripts take input: a name,

3
00:00:07,105 --> 00:00:07,577
a file,

4
00:00:07,577 --> 00:00:08,183
a number.

5
00:00:08,183 --> 00:00:11,081
Today you learn four ways to feed a script.

6
00:00:11,631 --> 00:00:12,288
Variables,

7
00:00:12,288 --> 00:00:13,340
to store values.

8
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:14,719
Positional arguments,

9
00:00:14,719 --> 00:00:17,479
to pass values in when you run the script.

10
00:00:17,479 --> 00:00:18,596
The read command,

11
00:00:18,596 --> 00:00:19,647
to ask the user.

12
00:00:19,647 --> 00:00:21,290
And command substitution,

13
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:23,983
to capture the output of another command.

14
00:00:24,533 --> 00:00:27,328
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

15
00:00:29,378 --> 00:00:30,561
Variables first.

16
00:00:30,561 --> 00:00:33,370
A variable is a named box for a value.

17
00:00:33,370 --> 00:00:34,626
Type: city=Paris.

18
00:00:34,626 --> 00:00:37,805
There are no spaces around the equals sign.

19
00:00:38,355 --> 00:00:39,372
Nothing printed.

20
00:00:39,372 --> 00:00:41,408
Read it back with a dollar sign.

21
00:00:41,408 --> 00:00:42,107
Type: echo,

22
00:00:42,107 --> 00:00:42,934
double quote,

23
00:00:42,934 --> 00:00:44,333
I live in dollar city,

24
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:45,160
double quote.

25
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:46,114
Paris.

26
00:00:46,114 --> 00:00:46,855
Now a trap.

27
00:00:46,855 --> 00:00:48,605
I want the word Parisland.

28
00:00:48,605 --> 00:00:49,346
Type: echo,

29
00:00:49,346 --> 00:00:50,221
double quote,

30
00:00:50,221 --> 00:00:51,299
dollar cityland,

31
00:00:51,299 --> 00:00:52,174
double quote.

32
00:00:52,724 --> 00:00:53,672
An empty line.

33
00:00:53,672 --> 00:00:56,920
The shell looked for a variable called cityland,

34
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:58,342
which does not exist.

35
00:00:58,342 --> 00:01:00,034
Mark where the name ends,

36
00:01:00,034 --> 00:01:00,846
with braces.

37
00:01:00,846 --> 00:01:01,590
Type: echo,

38
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:02,470
double quote,

39
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:02,944
dollar,

40
00:01:02,944 --> 00:01:03,688
open brace,

41
00:01:03,688 --> 00:01:04,027
city,

42
00:01:04,027 --> 00:01:04,839
close brace,

43
00:01:04,839 --> 00:01:05,177
land,

44
00:01:05,177 --> 00:01:06,057
double quote.

45
00:01:06,607 --> 00:01:07,340
Parisland.

46
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:10,416
Use braces whenever text touches the name.

47
00:01:10,416 --> 00:01:10,783
Next,

48
00:01:10,783 --> 00:01:11,955
a default value,

49
00:01:11,955 --> 00:01:14,226
for a variable that is not set.

50
00:01:14,226 --> 00:01:15,031
Type: echo,

51
00:01:15,031 --> 00:01:15,984
double quote,

52
00:01:15,984 --> 00:01:16,496
dollar,

53
00:01:16,496 --> 00:01:17,302
open brace,

54
00:01:17,302 --> 00:01:17,668
name,

55
00:01:17,668 --> 00:01:18,108
colon,

56
00:01:18,108 --> 00:01:18,474
dash,

57
00:01:18,474 --> 00:01:19,133
stranger,

58
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:20,012
close brace,

59
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:20,965
double quote.

60
00:01:21,515 --> 00:01:22,192
Stranger.

61
00:01:22,192 --> 00:01:24,601
The variable name was never set,

62
00:01:24,601 --> 00:01:26,407
so the default was used.

63
00:01:26,407 --> 00:01:28,063
One last trap: spaces.

64
00:01:28,063 --> 00:01:28,891
Type: city,

65
00:01:28,891 --> 00:01:29,343
space,

66
00:01:29,343 --> 00:01:29,870
equals,

67
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:30,322
space,

68
00:01:30,322 --> 00:01:30,773
Paris.

69
00:01:31,323 --> 00:01:33,610
The shell treated city as a command,

70
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:34,309
and failed.

71
00:01:34,309 --> 00:01:36,087
An assignment has no spaces.

72
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:36,977
Keep it tight.

73
00:01:39,027 --> 00:01:39,376
Next,

74
00:01:39,376 --> 00:01:40,839
positional arguments.

75
00:01:40,839 --> 00:01:44,325
They are the words you type after the script name.

76
00:01:44,325 --> 00:01:46,556
Here is a script that uses them.

77
00:01:46,556 --> 00:01:47,880
Type: cat greet.sh.

78
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:50,734
Dollar one is the first argument.

79
00:01:50,734 --> 00:01:53,107
Dollar hash is how many there are.

80
00:01:53,107 --> 00:01:54,852
Dollar at is all of them.

81
00:01:54,852 --> 00:01:56,318
Run it with one word.

82
00:01:56,318 --> 00:01:58,412
Type: dot slash greet dot S H,

83
00:01:58,412 --> 00:01:58,692
Sam.

84
00:01:59,242 --> 00:02:00,212
First is Sam,

85
00:02:00,212 --> 00:02:01,780
and the count is one.

86
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:02,975
Now three words.

87
00:02:02,975 --> 00:02:05,215
Type: dot slash greet dot S H,

88
00:02:05,215 --> 00:02:06,111
Sam Lee Kim.

89
00:02:06,661 --> 00:02:07,942
The count is three,

90
00:02:07,942 --> 00:02:09,628
and all three are listed.

91
00:02:09,628 --> 00:02:11,854
What if one argument has a space?

92
00:02:11,854 --> 00:02:12,461
Quote it,

93
00:02:12,461 --> 00:02:14,214
and it stays one argument.

94
00:02:14,214 --> 00:02:16,237
Type: dot slash greet dot S H,

95
00:02:16,237 --> 00:02:17,114
double quote,

96
00:02:17,114 --> 00:02:17,653
Sam Lee,

97
00:02:17,653 --> 00:02:18,530
double quote,

98
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:18,800
Kim.

99
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:20,615
First is Sam Lee,

100
00:02:20,615 --> 00:02:22,178
and the count is two.

101
00:02:22,178 --> 00:02:23,592
Quotes group words,

102
00:02:23,592 --> 00:02:24,708
just as before.

103
00:02:24,708 --> 00:02:27,016
Last case: no arguments at all.

104
00:02:27,016 --> 00:02:29,248
Type: dot slash greet dot S H.

105
00:02:29,798 --> 00:02:30,741
First is empty,

106
00:02:30,741 --> 00:02:32,123
and the count is zero.

107
00:02:32,123 --> 00:02:35,767
A good script checks the count before it uses an argument.

108
00:02:35,767 --> 00:02:37,841
You learn how in the next lesson.

109
00:02:39,891 --> 00:02:41,700
Third way: ask the user.

110
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:44,940
The read command waits for a line of input.

111
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:46,674
Here is a short script.

112
00:02:46,674 --> 00:02:47,955
Type: cat ask.sh.

113
00:02:48,505 --> 00:02:50,544
Read dash p prints a prompt,

114
00:02:50,544 --> 00:02:53,893
then stores what the user types in a variable.

115
00:02:53,893 --> 00:02:54,403
Run it,

116
00:02:54,403 --> 00:02:56,514
and then answer the question.

117
00:02:56,514 --> 00:02:58,553
Type: dot slash ask dot S H.

118
00:03:04,553 --> 00:03:05,145
Welcome,

119
00:03:05,145 --> 00:03:05,515
Maya.

120
00:03:05,515 --> 00:03:07,440
The script used my answer.

121
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:10,401
Scripts also run with no person present,

122
00:03:10,401 --> 00:03:12,622
so input can come from a pipe.

123
00:03:12,622 --> 00:03:13,733
Type: echo Lee,

124
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:14,103
pipe,

125
00:03:14,103 --> 00:03:15,732
dot slash ask dot S H.

126
00:03:16,282 --> 00:03:17,395
No prompt appeared,

127
00:03:17,395 --> 00:03:19,562
because there was no keyboard to ask.

128
00:03:19,562 --> 00:03:21,788
But the answer was read from the pipe.

129
00:03:21,788 --> 00:03:23,663
The same script works both ways.

130
00:03:25,713 --> 00:03:27,928
Fourth way: command substitution.

131
00:03:27,928 --> 00:03:30,009
It captures a command's output,

132
00:03:30,009 --> 00:03:31,352
so you can store it.

133
00:03:31,352 --> 00:03:33,097
Type: today equals dollar,

134
00:03:33,097 --> 00:03:33,634
bracket,

135
00:03:33,634 --> 00:03:35,514
date plus percent capital F,

136
00:03:35,514 --> 00:03:36,051
bracket.

137
00:03:36,051 --> 00:03:37,394
That format is year,

138
00:03:37,394 --> 00:03:37,796
month,

139
00:03:37,796 --> 00:03:38,065
day.

140
00:03:38,615 --> 00:03:39,721
Nothing printed.

141
00:03:39,721 --> 00:03:42,071
The output went into the variable.

142
00:03:42,071 --> 00:03:43,868
Use it inside a file name.

143
00:03:43,868 --> 00:03:44,628
Type: echo,

144
00:03:44,628 --> 00:03:45,526
double quote,

145
00:03:45,526 --> 00:03:47,807
backup dash dollar today dot tar,

146
00:03:47,807 --> 00:03:48,706
double quote.

147
00:03:49,256 --> 00:03:50,670
A dated file name,

148
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:52,163
built by the shell.

149
00:03:52,163 --> 00:03:53,499
Now count things.

150
00:03:53,499 --> 00:03:54,285
Type: L S,

151
00:03:54,285 --> 00:03:54,678
pipe,

152
00:03:54,678 --> 00:03:54,992
W C,

153
00:03:54,992 --> 00:03:55,542
dash l.

154
00:03:55,542 --> 00:03:56,957
That counts lines.

155
00:03:57,507 --> 00:03:58,478
Three files.

156
00:03:58,478 --> 00:03:59,935
Store that number.

157
00:03:59,935 --> 00:04:02,039
Type: count equals dollar,

158
00:04:02,039 --> 00:04:02,686
bracket,

159
00:04:02,686 --> 00:04:03,010
L S,

160
00:04:03,010 --> 00:04:03,415
pipe,

161
00:04:03,415 --> 00:04:03,738
W C,

162
00:04:03,738 --> 00:04:04,305
dash l,

163
00:04:04,305 --> 00:04:04,952
bracket.

164
00:04:05,502 --> 00:04:06,850
Again nothing printed.

165
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:08,627
Now print a sentence with it.

166
00:04:08,627 --> 00:04:09,301
Type: echo,

167
00:04:09,301 --> 00:04:10,097
double quote,

168
00:04:10,097 --> 00:04:11,874
There are dollar count files,

169
00:04:11,874 --> 00:04:12,670
double quote.

170
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,636
There are three files.

171
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:17,660
A script can now report facts about the system,

172
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:18,947
not just fixed text.

173
00:04:18,947 --> 00:04:20,942
The older style uses backticks.

174
00:04:20,942 --> 00:04:21,521
It works,

175
00:04:21,521 --> 00:04:23,580
but brackets are easier to read,

176
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:25,060
and they can be nested.

177
00:04:27,110 --> 00:04:31,697
Now put it all together in one script. Type: cat backup.sh.

178
00:04:32,247 --> 00:04:34,519
A default name with colon dash,

179
00:04:34,519 --> 00:04:36,938
a date from command substitution,

180
00:04:36,938 --> 00:04:37,964
and a message.

181
00:04:37,964 --> 00:04:39,724
Run it with no argument.

182
00:04:39,724 --> 00:04:41,996
Type: dot slash backup dot S H.

183
00:04:42,546 --> 00:04:44,452
It used the default name,

184
00:04:44,452 --> 00:04:44,834
data,

185
00:04:44,834 --> 00:04:46,130
and today's date.

186
00:04:46,130 --> 00:04:47,350
Now pass a name.

187
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,714
Type: dot slash backup dot S H,

188
00:04:49,714 --> 00:04:50,247
photos.

189
00:04:50,797 --> 00:04:56,131
Photos. Same script, different input. That is why scripts use variables and arguments.

190
00:04:58,181 --> 00:04:59,504
Four myths to clear up.

191
00:05:00,054 --> 00:05:03,480
Myth one: spaces around the equals sign are fine.

192
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:03,690
No.

193
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:05,438
Spaces make it a command,

194
00:05:05,438 --> 00:05:06,347
and it fails.

195
00:05:06,897 --> 00:05:09,484
Myth two: dollar one is the script name.

196
00:05:09,484 --> 00:05:09,678
No.

197
00:05:09,678 --> 00:05:10,971
That is dollar zero.

198
00:05:10,971 --> 00:05:13,105
Dollar one is the first argument.

199
00:05:13,655 --> 00:05:16,792
Myth three: an unquoted variable is always fine.

200
00:05:16,792 --> 00:05:18,557
If the value holds a space,

201
00:05:18,557 --> 00:05:19,211
it splits.

202
00:05:19,211 --> 00:05:19,799
Quote it.

203
00:05:20,349 --> 00:05:25,149
Myth four: read only works with a keyboard. It also reads from pipes and files.

204
00:05:25,699 --> 00:05:28,152
Set it. Pass it. Ask for it. Capture it.

205
00:05:30,202 --> 00:05:34,767
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

206
00:05:35,317 --> 00:05:39,349
Assign and print a variable, with no spaces around the equals sign.

207
00:05:39,899 --> 00:05:42,886
Use braces, and a default value with colon dash.

208
00:05:43,436 --> 00:05:46,785
Name dollar zero, dollar one, dollar hash, and dollar at.

209
00:05:47,335 --> 00:05:49,469
Quote an argument that contains a space.

210
00:05:50,019 --> 00:05:53,133
Use read to take input, from a keyboard and from a pipe.

211
00:05:53,683 --> 00:05:56,990
Capture a command's output with a dollar sign and brackets.

212
00:05:57,540 --> 00:05:59,161
To pass, you need all six.

213
00:06:01,211 --> 00:06:06,011
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine. Type every command yourself.

214
00:06:06,561 --> 00:06:10,593
One: set two variables, and print them in one sentence, using braces.

215
00:06:11,143 --> 00:06:14,516
Two: write a script that prints its first argument,

216
00:06:14,516 --> 00:06:15,177
the count,

217
00:06:15,177 --> 00:06:16,368
and all arguments.

218
00:06:16,368 --> 00:06:17,492
Run it with zero,

219
00:06:17,492 --> 00:06:17,756
one,

220
00:06:17,756 --> 00:06:19,079
and three arguments.

221
00:06:19,629 --> 00:06:24,088
Three: run it with an argument that contains a space, with quotes and without.

222
00:06:24,638 --> 00:06:27,580
Four: write a script that asks your name with read,

223
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:28,445
and greets you.

224
00:06:28,445 --> 00:06:29,310
Run it by hand,

225
00:06:29,310 --> 00:06:30,291
then from a pipe.

226
00:06:30,841 --> 00:06:34,402
Five: store today's date and the number of files in your folder,

227
00:06:34,402 --> 00:06:36,238
and print one sentence with both.

228
00:06:36,788 --> 00:06:39,840
Six: write a backup name script with a default value,

229
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:42,143
and run it with and without an argument.

230
00:06:42,693 --> 00:06:45,999
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

231
00:07:01,550 --> 00:07:02,425
Quick check.

232
00:07:02,975 --> 00:07:04,425
What does dollar hash hold?

233
00:07:04,975 --> 00:07:08,133
How do you keep an argument with a space as one argument?

234
00:07:08,683 --> 00:07:10,837
Which command asks the user for input?

235
00:07:11,387 --> 00:07:13,457
What does a dollar sign with brackets do?

236
00:07:14,007 --> 00:07:19,703
Objective covered: MC05-O02.

237
00:07:20,253 --> 00:07:22,322
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

238
00:07:32,872 --> 00:07:36,904
Next lesson: conditions, tests, case statements, loops and functions.

239
00:07:37,454 --> 00:07:40,782
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
